      "settings": {
        "section_anchor_id": "gardens",
        "eyebrow": "Gardens",
        "heading": "Palms, elms and a Barossa courtyard.",
        "body": "<p>The most iconic landscape in the valley is the Avenue of Palms: a five-kilometre trail of Canary Island Date Palms, planted by Seppeltsfield workers during the Great Depression. More than 2,000 palms now line Seppeltsfield Road.</p><p>In the village, a citrus courtyard and cascading water feature welcome guests to Cellar Door, with palms and crepe myrtle shading the FINO dining space. Elm Walk, planted by the Seppelt family around 1900, crosses Greenock Creek — a cool hideaway in a Barossa summer, under eucalypts, pines and poplars, with kookaburras, magpies and, at dawn or dusk, the occasional kangaroo.</p>",
        "link_label": "Plan a day on the estate",
